## Select the outcome

`measurement_text` is the cleaned primary result column. Use it to select the main numeric outcome for the canonical endpoint or explicitly named assay readout. `support_text` may show that same outcome in its source-printed form, identify the selected coefficient, complete its unit, or resolve what its denominator means. Do not replace the primary outcome with an incidental support number.

Do not select an administered dose, assay concentration, exposure duration, timepoint, sample size, identifier, p-value, control value, comparator value, cytotoxicity observation, or another assay condition. A number is eligible only when it quantifies the row's canonical endpoint or explicitly named readout. Several equally selected outcomes, or an unresolved conflict about the selected value or unit, are `unsure`.

Qualitative calls are handled outside this extraction. A positive, negative, detected, increased, or decreased statement without a selected numeric outcome is `unavailable`.

## Choose the status

Use `ok` for one finite point with a complete, source-supported unit or denominator. An endpoint-defined ratio, mutant frequency, induction factor, response percentage, count per plate or culture, or other named readout remains self-contained when its denominator is fixed by the source metric.

Use `relative` when the selected numeric claim depends on an experiment-specific comparator and the endpoint or named readout does not define that comparison. Examples include a fold difference from a particular control, treatment, genotype, dose arm, or other compound. A qualitative comparison without a numeric coefficient is `unavailable`.

Use `unsure` for a bound or range without a point, unresolved arithmetic, multiple equally selected values, an unresolved unit, or contradictory source statements. Use `unavailable` when the selected outcome is absent or nonnumeric.

## Preserve the report

Return a finite plain decimal with optional sign and decimal point. Remove valid thousands separators. Preserve the stated central point when it is accompanied by a spread or interval, but do not turn a bound or range into a point. Do not calculate, average, convert, round, infer a logarithmic base, or impose a plausibility cutoff.

A power of ten printed with the selected value stays explicit in the unit: `3.2 x 10^-6` with `events/cell` becomes measurement `3.2` and unit `10^-6 events/cell`. When `measurement_text` contains the expanded equivalent but `support_text` explicitly prints the selected outcome as a coefficient times a power, copy the printed coefficient and keep the power in the unit. Keep an expanded decimal only when the source context itself reports that decimal without a printed equivalent. Never reverse or silently apply a printed scale.

{% if has_unit_column %}A nonempty `unit_text` is authoritative. Copy it exactly unless the selected value explicitly supplies a missing scale or unit component. When it lists units for multiple reported values, retain only the unit component attached to the selected value. Use support only to complete a genuinely incomplete unit. An unresolved conflict is `unsure`.
{% else %}Read the selected outcome's unit or fixed denominator from the measurement and support text. If neither supplies one, return `unsure` rather than inventing a unit.
{% endif %}
Use `%`, `ratio`, `fraction`, `fold`, or a count denominator only when the source explicitly prints or defines it. Do not use an endpoint name, assay name, species, tissue, dose, or condition as a unit.

Additional fields provide context for the selected measurement. They are not alternative measurement columns and must not override conflicting primary text.
